  "turn": {
    "pericope": "Matthew 11:16-19, 25-30",
    "refKey": "Matt.11.16-Matt.11.30",
    "gravity": "The day sets a humble king on a donkey (Zechariah), the Lord who is gracious and slow to anger (the Psalm), and Paul gasping \"I do not do the good I want\" (Romans 7) — and then Jesus: \"Come to me, all you who are weary.\" The nerve: the gospel arrives not as one more demand on the competent but as rest for the worn-out and welcome for the unimpressive.",
    "trap": "\"Come to me\" shrinks to a self-care slogan — Jesus as a place to relax — or the yoke gets reloaded into a heavier law where real Christians just try harder. Either way the strange logic is lost: that the road to rest runs through a yoke, and the door in is to become a child.",
    "hinge": "He promises rest and then hands you a yoke. A burden that is somehow light, a surrender that is somehow freedom. You cannot preach the comfort without the yoke, or the yoke without the comfort.",
    "doors": [
      {
        "landing": "The easy yoke",
        "claim": "What looks like another demand is rest — the weary are not told to try harder but to trade the world's crushing yoke for a gentle one.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Gregory the Great",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "It is a cruel yoke and a hard weight of servitude to be subject to the things of time, ambitious of earth, clinging to falling things — that is the burden Christ lifts."
          },
          {
            "author": "John Wesley",
            "tradition": "wesleyan",
            "work": "Explanatory Notes",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "My yoke is easy — or rather gracious, sweet, benign, delightful; and my burden, contrary to those of men, is ease, liberty, and honour."
          }
        ],
        "say": "You are carrying a yoke already; the only question is whose — come and trade it for the one that fits.",
        "do": "Name one heavy thing they are dragging that Christ never asked them to carry, and have them lay it down this week."
      },
      {
        "landing": "Hidden from the wise",
        "claim": "The kingdom is not opened by being clever — it is hidden from the proud and shown to children, so the way in is down, not up.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Augustine",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The wise and understanding here are the proud; for who are the babes to whom it is revealed but the humble?"
          },
          {
            "author": "Gregory the Great",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "He says not 'to the foolish' but 'to babes,' showing that he condemns pride, not understanding."
          }
        ],
        "say": "You will not think your way into this; it opens to the ones small enough to receive it.",
        "do": "Move the competent and capable in the room to one place they need to stop managing God and become a child again."
      },
      {
        "landing": "We piped and you did not dance",
        "claim": "This generation rejects every tune — John's fast and Jesus' feast alike — always finding a reason to refuse God in whatever form God comes.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Jerome",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "If fasting pleased you, why not John? If fulness, why not the Son of Man? One you called possessed, the other a glutton — no form of God would do."
          },
          {
            "author": "John Chrysostom",
            "tradition": "greek-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "I and John came by opposite ways to do the same thing, like two hunters driving the same quarry from two sides — what excuse then is left?"
          }
        ],
        "say": "Notice how endlessly inventive we are at saying no to God — the austere prophet too grim, the joyful Lord too loose.",
        "do": "Expose one excuse the hearer keeps ready for not answering God, and let it fall."
      }
    ],
    "subtract": "Three doors, three landings — rest for the weary, humility for the wise, the exposed excuse. Choose the one your people most need this week and let the other two go.",
